# Results summary

Real-time self-adaptive agentic load forecasting: UCI ElectricityLoadDiagrams, 8 clients, hourly, prequential (test-then-train) evaluation on the last 35% of each series (9,207 hours per client). All numbers are means over the 8 clients on the test block; every setting was chosen on the validation block first. Generated by `make_results_summary.py`.

**Final system:** a pool of 5 forecasters (seasonal-naive, Holt-Winters, SARIMA, LightGBM, online linear), combined by MELO (Wang, Goude & Yao 2026). On top sits our agentic layer: ADWIN + Page-Hinkley drift detection, follow-up refits at +24/+72/+168 h after an alarm, and a contextual serving agent that re-weights MELO and the models each hour.

## 1. Real test data

| Method | MAE | RMSE | CV-RMSE % | MAPE % | refits/wk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Equal-weight ensemble | 79.51 | 117.90 | 7.59 | 6.14 | 3.0 |
| Validation-chosen single model | 73.54 | 115.17 | 7.47 | 5.19 | 3.0 |
| FTL | 72.84 | 111.82 | 7.36 | 5.20 | 3.0 |
| Hedge / EWA | 70.98 | 108.66 | 7.11 | 5.13 | 3.0 |
| DWM | 68.69 | 106.53 | 6.96 | 4.98 | 3.0 |
| MLpol | 68.17 | 103.22 | 6.55 | 5.02 | 3.0 |
| MELO (2026) | 66.50 | 101.37 | 6.47 | 4.82 | 3.0 |
| Initial controller (top-3 blend + bandit) | 69.84 | 106.54 | 6.74 | 5.01 | 3.4 |
| Initial controller, refit every hour | 68.23 | 103.14 | 6.49 | 4.93 | 504.0 |
| MELO, refit every hour | 65.54 | 100.19 | 6.26 | 4.68 | 504.0 |
| **Ours: MELO + drift + follow-up refits** | 66.58 | 101.26 | 6.33 | 4.80 | 4.4 |
| **Ours + contextual agent (final)** | 66.74 | 101.31 | 6.34 | 4.70 | 4.3 |

Cost of the final system: 31.5 ms per hourly step on this laptop. The agentic logic (drift, MELO, agent) is 1.9% of that; the rest is the models. Refitting every hour costs about 2,850 ms per step.

## 2. Injected demand shifts (MAPE % / CV-RMSE %)

Two events per client per scenario: level (+30%, later back), ramp (+30% over 4 weeks, later back), amplitude (daily swing x1.6, later back), temporary (+40% for one week, twice). Daily refit = refitting the models every 24 h.

| Method | none | level | ramp | amplitude | temporary |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| MELO (weekly refit) | 4.82 / 6.47 | 4.90 / 6.61 | 4.86 / 6.50 | 8.70 / 7.68 | 4.94 / 6.80 |
| Ours (no agent) | 4.80 / 6.33 | 4.86 / 6.45 | 4.82 / 6.39 | 9.20 / 7.58 | 4.89 / 6.56 |
| **Ours + agent** | 4.70 / 6.34 | 4.75 / 6.45 | 4.74 / 6.40 | 8.38 / 7.59 | 4.79 / 6.58 |
| MELO, daily refit (21 refits/wk) | - | 4.84 / 6.48 | 4.80 / 6.39 | 8.95 / 7.59 | 4.88 / 6.57 |

Shift detection (proposed system): level 11/16 events caught, median delay 45 h; ramp 7/16 events caught, median delay 119 h; amplitude 9/16 events caught, median delay 92 h; temporary 13/16 events caught, median delay 17 h. False alarms about 0.20 per week.

The amplitude MAPE is inflated by one client (MT_135), whose near-zero hours become almost zero under the stretch. CV-RMSE is the fairer measure there.

## 3. Statistical significance

Diebold-Mariano test on hourly losses (Newey-West, lag 24 h) for each of 40 series (8 clients x 5 scenarios), plus a Wilcoxon signed-rank test across the series.

| Comparison | loss | better in | sig. better | sig. worse | Wilcoxon p |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| proposed + agent vs MELO alone | absolute | 21/40 | 17 | 5 | 0.0134 |
| proposed + agent vs MELO alone | squared | 22/40 | 3 | 1 | 0.0372 |
| proposed + agent vs MELO alone | abs. % error | 20/40 | 17 | 8 | 0.0248 |
| proposed (no agent) vs MELO alone | absolute | 29/40 | 14 | 0 | 0.00457 |
| proposed (no agent) vs MELO alone | squared | 30/40 | 7 | 0 | 0.00586 |
| proposed (no agent) vs MELO alone | abs. % error | 26/40 | 13 | 0 | 0.0174 |
| proposed + agent vs proposed (no agent) | absolute | 19/40 | 12 | 9 | 0.313 |
| proposed + agent vs proposed (no agent) | squared | 10/40 | 1 | 3 | 0.253 |
| proposed + agent vs proposed (no agent) | abs. % error | 19/40 | 11 | 7 | 0.326 |

## 4. Main findings

1. **Final system vs MELO (2026):** lower MAPE in every scenario (real data 4.70 vs 4.82), and significantly better overall. Its MAE is slightly higher: the agent trades a little absolute accuracy for better percentage accuracy in low-load hours.
2. **Drift detection with follow-up refits** is significantly better than MELO alone, and never significantly worse in any of the 40 series. CV-RMSE improves 1-3.4% in every scenario. It matches MELO refitted daily, with about a fifth of the refits.
3. **The contextual agent's extra gain over (2) is not significant** (p 0.25-0.33). It helps some clients and hurts others.
4. **What did not work:**
    - refitting on post-shift data only (one week of data is too little for SARIMA / LightGBM)
    - resetting MELO after alarms
    - learning the agent from squared errors
    - the initial top-3 inverse-error blend, which every adaptive combiner beat
5. **Bugs found and fixed along the way:**
    - a Holt-Winters seasonal-phase error
    - SARIMA forecasting up to 168 h blind
    - a drift detector that behaved as a weekly timer
    - a recovery metric that always returned 1 h

